#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f9502e-19c3-58bb-98da-1562e0b0dfd0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations"}] :question/id #uuid "de1fa48e-853a-5aa4-ab32-f23540ef1dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14598" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $\\mathbb{Z}$?\n\n1. $a \\not \\mid b$\n2. $a \\,R\\, b$ if $a - b$ is a multiple of $7$\n3. $a\\, R \\,b$ if $a \\equiv (b - 1) \\; (\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x \\in A$, $x \\, R\\, x$.\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y \\in A$, if $x \\,R\\, y$, then $y \\,R\\, x$.\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z \\in A$, if $x \\,R\\, y$ and $y \\,R\\, z$, then $x \\,R\\, z$.\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $1 \\mid 1$.\n- Relation II is an equivalence relation. It is reflexive because for all $a \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, we have that $a - a=0$ is a multiple of $7$. It is symmetric because for all $a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, if $a - b$ is a multiple of $7$, then $b - a=-(a-b)$ is also a multiple of $7$.It is transitive because for all $a, b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, if $a - b=7n$ and $b - c=7m$, where $n, m$ are arbitrary integers, then\n$a - c = (a - b) + (b - c)=7n+7m=7(n+m)$\nThis means that if $a - b$ is a multiple of $7$ and $b - c$ is a multiple of $7$ then $a-c$ is also a multiple of $7$.\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $1 \\not\\equiv (1 - 1) \\; (\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151845 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "6b0a0a3a-7079-5798-b2b3-14f7165ade81" :question/math-academy-id "e-14599" :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A=\\{\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma\\}$ defined as\n\n$R=\\big\\{(\\alpha, \\alpha), (\\beta,\\beta), (\\gamma,\\gamma), (\\beta,\\gamma), (\\gamma,\\beta)\\big\\}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $R$ is reflexive\n2. $R$ is symmetric\n3. $R$ is transitive\n4. $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x \\in A$, $x \\, R\\, x$.\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y \\in A$, if $x \\,R\\, y$, then $y \\,R\\, x$.\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z \\in A$, if $x \\,R\\, y$ and $y \\,R\\, z$, then $x \\,R\\, z$.\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive because $(\\alpha, \\alpha), (\\beta,\\beta), (\\gamma,\\gamma) \\in R$.$\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x) \\in R$ whenever $(x,y) \\in R$. $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in \\{\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma\\}$, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R$, then $(x,z)\\in R$. Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: $({\\color{blue}\\beta},{\\color{red}\\gamma}) \\in R$ and $({\\color{red}\\gamma},{\\color{blue}\\beta}) \\in R$, and also $({\\color{blue}\\beta},{\\color{blue}\\beta}) \\in R$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$$({\\color{blue}\\gamma},{\\color{red}\\beta}) \\in R$ and $({\\color{red}\\beta},{\\color{blue}\\gamma}) \\in R$, and also $({\\color{blue}\\gamma},{\\color{blue}\\gamma}) \\in R$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Since $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation.$\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b70dd396-b01a-51e6-a52c-8ff729f48c45" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Standard Relation Is an Equivalence"}] :question/id #uuid "de6949c6-850c-511e-8bea-bb246972cda4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14695" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $\\mathcal{P}({B})$, where $B=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$.\n\n$X \\,R\\, Y \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad X \\subseteq Y$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $R$ is reflexive\n2. $R$ is symmetric\n3. $R$ is transitive\n4. $R$ is an equivalence relation" :question/worked-solution "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x \\in A$, $x \\, R\\, x$.\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y \\in A$, if $x \\,R\\, y$, then $y \\,R\\, x$.\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z \\in A$, if $x \\,R\\, y$ and $y \\,R\\, z$, then $x \\,R\\, z$.\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive. For all $X \\in \\mathcal{P}({B})$, $X \\, \\subseteq \\, X$.\n- The relation $R$ is not symmetric. For example, $\\{1\\} \\subseteq \\{1,2\\}$ but $\\{1,2\\} \\not \\subseteq \\{1\\}$.\n- The relation $R$ is transitive. For all $X,Y,Z \\in \\mathcal{P}({B})$, if $X \\subseteq Y$ and $Y \\subseteq Z$, then $X \\subseteq Z$.\n- The relation $R$ is not an equivalence relation since it's not symmetric.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b70dd396-b01a-51e6-a52c-8ff729f48c45" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Standard Relation Is an Equivalence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943268f0-e1b4-513a-949a-2d21d36b144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149557} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3b8483-7822-5961-8352-009c78743961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149558} {:answer/id #uuid "08f98ec1-18ee-57a6-969c-0d18c2628d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149559} {:answer/id #uuid "34dc1e82-49c3-5dba-b2c5-d8a52a7f4307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149560} {:answer/id #uuid "370eafa0-4bc5-59db-869d-c3a8e48711d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149561}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3d5c8a-9c69-53c4-bf41-d1986e372cfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149556}] :question/id #uuid "a2a24748-f2b1-5dae-9e25-ddb47fe6a36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-214134" :question/problem "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A$ containing all possible triangles in $R^{2}$.\n\n$xRy⇔x≅y$\n\nRecall that $x≅y$ means that $x$ and $y$ are congruent.\n\nWhich of the following are true?\n\n1. $R$ is reflexive\n2. $R$ is symmetric\n3. $R$ is transitive\n4. $R$ is an equivalence relation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "968df6ec-e87e-5770-9c11-e7c8d8a27e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Relations on Finite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d2717-83d7-51b0-a227-fa1fc6809409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149569} {:answer/id #uuid "30eef23f-1a55-5ac4-8a57-9c63c6f949e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149570} {:answer/id #uuid "5b128d60-e594-5aa5-bff5-a1e2fa17357d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149571} {:answer/id #uuid "66c8be3b-ec27-54c3-a77d-bf9d20dd0b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149572} {:answer/id #uuid "7410e289-bd9b-5799-8292-33605aba5faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149573}] :answer-field/id #uuid "692a1b0c-9534-5975-bd7a-3a92a99093ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149568}] :question/id #uuid "14a51040-e179-5b66-9e0b-fca0e478f23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214171" :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A = {0, 1, 2}$ defined as\n$R = {(1, 1), (0, 2), (2, 0)}$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $R$ is reflexive\n2. $R$ is symmetric\n3. $R$ is transitive\n4. $R$ is an equivalence relation"}]}
